From 90fb2c0f0165d10bd9f8fd8d43a45865c1aa31ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 22 Jun 2026 13:56:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Prototype: fuse oxDNA hbond+xstk Kokkos kernels and use acosf
 (FP32)

Two GPU-oriented optimizations for the screened-pair oxDNA Kokkos pair styles,
prototyped and verified with the Kokkos Serial backend on the oxDNA2 duplex2
example (examples/PACKAGES/cgdna/.../oxDNA2/duplex2).

#1 Fused hbond + xstk kernel
  The hydrogen-bonding (F1) and cross-stacking (F2) screened-pair kernels both
  act on the same base-site separation (COM + 0.4*nx) and share the same six
  interaction angles theta1..4,7,8. They previously ran as two separate
  edge-parallel kernels, each recomputing the base-site geometry and all six
  acos() angles over the same screened pair list. The new fused kernel
  (TagPairOxdnaHbXstkFused in pair_oxdna_hbond_kokkos) computes that geometry
  and the six acosf() values once, then evaluates both interactions. Each term
  self-cuts via its own radial modulation (F1 with the hbond cutoff, F2 with the
  xstk cutoff), so the differing hbond/xstk radial cutoffs on the shared screened
  list are handled correctly. The xstk style skips its own screened pass when
  fusion is active; the combined energy is tallied by the hbond style, so the
  total potential energy is unchanged.

#2 acos -> acosf in the screened-pair kernels
  The screened/GPU kernels already use sqrtf/expf FP32 intrinsics; switch the
  six per-pair acos() calls to acosf() for consistency.

Verification harness (prototype only, default OFF):
  - OXDNA_KK_FORCE_SCREENED=1 runs the screened/GPU pair path on the host so it
    can be exercised under Kokkos Serial.
  - OXDNA_KK_FUSE_HBXSTK=1 enables the fused hbond+xstk kernel.
  Neither variable affects normal GPU execution or the default host path.

Serial results (duplex2, 1000 steps, total potential energy):
  - Fused kernel reproduces the separate hbond+xstk screened kernels
    bit-for-bit at every printed step.
  - The screened/acosf path (and hence the fused kernel) tracks the original
    double-precision per-atom path to ~1e-7, i.e. only the expected FP32
    intrinsic difference.
